items.brief:
What is wrong today
At city age some cultures build almost only one kind of house. A Suncoast city grown to 600 people has 50 homes and every one of them is a 'corner' house (84 of 84 at 1000 people). A Clayborn city is about 90% corner houses.

Required behaviour
1. Single-parcel city homes of every culture show a real mix. For each culture in CULTURE_IDS, build 80 homes, i = 0 to 79, with
     houseForm({ age: 'city', level: 3, wealth: (i % 10) / 10, seed: `t:home-${i}`, culture, plot: [5.2, 4.6], district })
   where district cycles 'residential', 'residential', 'market', 'core' (that list at index i % 4). Their .type values (HouseType) include at least three distinct types, and no single type is more than 65% of the 80.
2. Real grown cities show the mix too. For 'suncoast' and for 'clayborn': realm = createRealms(); id = resolveGrowTarget(`culture:${culture}`); s = growRealmTo(realm, id, 600). Take the completed homes, the entries of s.lots with buildingType 'house' and tier > 0 (there are at least 30), and read each one's type with realmHouseForm(s, lot).type. They include at least three distinct types, and no type is more than 70% of those homes.

Every existing typology rule still holds
- houseForm is deterministic: the same input gives a deep-equal result (for example { age: 'city', level: 3, wealth: .7, seed: 'x:home-9', culture: 'suncoast', plot: [5.2, 4.6], district: 'core' }).
- Take n homes houseForm({ age, level, wealth: (i % 10) / 10, seed: `t:home-${i}`, culture }) with no plot and no district (level 2, n = 60 and culture 'riverfolk' unless stated):
  - every hamlet home is a 'hut', 'cottage' or 'family' house;
  - a riverfolk city includes 'townhouse', 'corner' and 'manor';
  - frostholm villages have more 'longhouse' homes than riverfolk villages;
  - crownmarch towns include at least one 'row'; frostholm towns include none.
- Take 30 homes houseForm({ age: 'city', level: 3, wealth: .95, seed: `d:${i}`, culture, district, plot }):
  - riverfolk 'market' homes have more forms with shopFront set than riverfolk 'residential' homes;
  - riverfolk 'farm' homes are all 'longhouse' or 'row';
  - for every culture in CULTURE_IDS, on plots [5.2, 4.6], [6.4, 5] and [4, 4], in the 'residential', 'market' and 'core' districts, no home is a 'manor';
  - riverfolk 'residential' homes on a [7.4, 6] plot include at least one 'manor'.
- Every form fits its plot, for every age in AGES, every culture, levels 1 to 3, any wealth and seed, districts 'residential', 'market' and 'core', with a [5.2, 4.6] plot and with no plot (then the plot counts as [6.2, 5]):
  - footprint[0] <= plot width + 0.05 and footprint[1] <= plot depth + 0.05;
  - every mass m in masses has |m.x| + m.w / 2 <= footprint[0] / 2 + 0.1 and |m.z| + m.d / 2 <= footprint[1] / 2 + 0.1;
  - the door is on the front face: |door.z - (masses[0].z + masses[0].d / 2)| < 0.11.
- A culture uses a bounded set of templates: for cultures 'riverfolk', 'suncoast', 'frostholm' and 'crownmarch', every age in AGES, and 24 homes each (level 2, the n-homes recipe above), the set of `${culture}:${houseFormKey(form)}` keys has fewer than 160 entries.

Existing tests that pass today must keep passing unchanged.

What is wrong today
growRealmTo(realm, id, population) is the ?grow path, and it is also how established and discovered towns get the buildings for the population they advertise. It leaves people homeless, and the homeless camp around the council (the 'plaza crowding' that was deferred earlier, '29 campers without housing'):
- The nearest Highland town at 600 people has housing 504 (96 campers).
- The nearest Suncoast town at 600 has housing 568.
- Many frontier towns at 1000 have 912 to 992 places.
- Growing a town twice (Willowbank to 100, then to 600) ends at 548.
The game promises that an established town shows enough buildings for the population it already advertises.

Required behaviour
The towns: the core towns 'alder', 'willow' and 'cairn', plus resolveGrowTarget(`culture:${c}`) for every culture c in CULTURE_IDS (13 distinct RealmIds in all). For each of them, on a fresh realm from createRealms(), after s = growRealmTo(realm, id, pop) for each pop in 128, 300, 600 and 1000:
- s.population is pop;
- settlementHousing(s) >= pop, and s.housing >= pop;
- no entry of s.residents has homeId 'camp'.

The same holds when a town is grown in steps on one realm, checked after the last step:
- 'willow' to 100, then to 600;
- 'alder' to 128, then to 1000;
- the Highland town (resolveGrowTarget('culture:highland')) to 300, then to 600;
- the Suncoast town (resolveGrowTarget('culture:suncoast')) to 150, then to 1000.

Existing behaviour stays intact:
- growRealmTo(realm, 'alder', NaN) still returns null and leaves the realm exactly as it was (JSON.stringify(realm) is unchanged);
- a crowded core valley loses no lot, and invalid saved ids are still dropped;
- existing tests that pass today must keep passing.
